  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1020 PM MDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nMain upper high remains over the Mexican Sonoran Desert. Plenty of \nrecycled moisture remains under this high over southern Arizona and \nNew Mexico. Possible short waves over eastern Arizona helped kick \noff convection over the western CWA this evening. Significant drying \nout at low levels (sfc dewpoints) occurred over southern New Mexico \nfrom the RG Valley east-handled well by the HRRR. This ended any \nchance of storms in this area tonight. \n\nSaturday and Sunday...upper high slowly drifts east across New \nMexico over the souther Plains by Sunday. Dewpoints do recover some, \nbut still noticeably drier over much of the central and eastern CWA. \nBest chances for thunderstorms both days will be over the mountains \n(mainly Black Range) and areas west of the RG Valley. Temperatures \nwill remain well above normal and the lowlands could see their last \ntriple digit readings of the year. \n\nMonday and beyond...upper high continues east as far as eastern \nTennessee by Tuesday, then begins retrograding back, reaching the\nlower Mississippi Valley Thursday and then over Texas by Friday. \nThis means from Monday into next weekend, there will be good-to \nexcellent chances of rain for most of the CWA. The key will be how\nstrong of ridging noses westward toward New Mexico from the upper\nhigh. Models have been inconsistent with this. If this ridging \ntakes place, moisture will be limited mostly to the western half \nof the CWA; if not then most of the CWA will see some rain. \nWednesday through Saturday PWs increase to 1.2-1.4 inches so flood\npotential likely to increase during this period.",
